synthesis.electron-positron-to-muon-pair.formal:
Signature $(+,-,-,-)$ in $\Cl(1,3)$, the Clifford product in place of the matrix product, traces $4\langle\cdot\rangle_{0}$, the units of the Feynman rules (the field theory's unit $I_{4}$) outside the product (\claimref{synthesis.one-loop-qed-in-the-spacetime-algebra}), the vertex of \claimref{field-theory.coupled-system-tree-level}, the free fields of \claimref{field-theory.second-quantisation}, and the on-shell external states of Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-one-loop-integrals-and-pauli-villars-regulators}(v). Let $p_{1}, p_{2}$ be the incoming electron and positron, $p_{3}, p_{4}$ the outgoing muon and antimuon, $s = Q(p_{1} + p_{2})$, $\beta$ the muon speed in the centre-of-mass frame.
\begin{enumerate}
\item (LSZ; proved.) If $Q(p) = m^{2}$ then $(p + m)^{2} = 2m(p + m)$, $(p - m)^{2} = -2m(p - m)$, $(p + m)(p - m) = 0$, and $4\langle p \pm m\rangle_{0} = \pm 4m$: for $m \neq 0$, $(p + m)/(2m)$ is an idempotent of trace two. The propagator $S(p) = (p + m)/(Q(p) - m^{2})$ has residue $p + m$ at the pole, so after amputation and multiplication by $Z_{2}^{1/2}$ per leg (the reduction formula \cite{LSZ1955}, Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-lsz-reduction-ward-identity-and-the-second-qed-beta-coefficient}(i)) the sums over the two spin states of an outgoing particle and an incoming antiparticle are the multivectors $p + m$ and $p - m$.
\item (Tree; computed.) $\tfrac14\sum|M|^{2} = \tfrac{e^{4}}{4s^{2}}\,4\langle(p_{2} - m_{e})\gamma^{\mu}(p_{1} + m_{e})\gamma^{\nu}\rangle_{0}\,4\langle(p_{3} + m_{\mu})\gamma_{\mu}(p_{4} - m_{\mu})\gamma_{\nu}\rangle_{0} = \tfrac{8e^{4}}{s^{2}}[(p_{1}\cdot p_{3})(p_{2}\cdot p_{4}) + (p_{1}\cdot p_{4})(p_{2}\cdot p_{3}) + m_{\mu}^{2}\,p_{1}\cdot p_{2} + m_{e}^{2}\,p_{3}\cdot p_{4} + 2m_{e}^{2}m_{\mu}^{2}]$. For massless leptons $d\sigma/d\Omega = \tfrac{\alpha^{2}}{4s}(1 + \cos^{2}\theta)$ and $\sigma = \tfrac{4\pi\alpha^{2}}{3s}$; for $m_{e} = 0$, $\sigma = \tfrac{4\pi\alpha^{2}}{3s}\cdot\tfrac{\beta(3 - \beta^{2})}{2}$ \cite{PeskinSchroeder1995}.
\item (One loop; computed, the optical-theorem reading argued.) The longitudinal part $q_{\mu}q_{\nu}$ of the dressed photon vanishes between the on-shell currents ($q = p_{1} + p_{2}$), so the polarisation insertions resum to $M_{\mathrm{tree}}/(1 - \hat\Pi(s))$ with $\hat\Pi(s) = -\tfrac{2\alpha}{\pi}\int_{0}^{1}x(1-x)\ln\frac{m^{2}}{m^{2} - x(1-x)s - I_{4}0}\,dx$ (numbers read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_{4}\}$, $\mathrm{Im}$ the coefficient of $I_{4}$) for a loop fermion of mass $m$ (\claimref{synthesis.one-loop-qed-in-the-spacetime-algebra}(2), subtracted at $s = 0$; the subtracted function does not depend on the regulator, and the loop has no internal photon; with no regulator and no Krein form it is the extension of \claimref{synthesis.one-loop-vacuum-polarisation-extends-without-a-regulator-and-ward-preserving-extensions-differ-only-by-the-renormalisation-constants}(ii)). Its absorptive part is $\mathrm{Im}\,\hat\Pi(s + I_{4}0) = -2\alpha\int_{(1-\beta)/2}^{(1+\beta)/2}x(1-x)\,dx = -\tfrac{\alpha}{3}\cdot\tfrac{\beta(3 - \beta^{2})}{2}$, $\beta = (1 - 4m^{2}/s)^{1/2}$, and this equals $-s\,\sigma_{\mathrm{tree}}(e^{+}e^{-} \to f\bar f)/(4\pi\alpha)$ at $m_{e} = 0$: the cut of the photon self-energy is the tree rate (the s-channel optical-theorem relation for the photon self-energy; full unitarity at order $e^{4}$ also involves other channels, not computed). For $s \gg m^{2}$, $\mathrm{Re}\,\hat\Pi(s) = \tfrac{\alpha}{3\pi}(\ln(s/m^{2}) - \tfrac53) + O(m^{2}/s)$. Under the Krein-form premise \claimref{synthesis.krein-form-premise-indefinite-inner-product-for-gauge-fields} (the vertex and self-energy loops carry an internal photon), $Z_{1} = Z_{2}$ (Ward's identity, Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-lsz-reduction-ward-identity-and-the-second-qed-beta-coefficient}(ii)) and the vertex counterterms cancel the leg factors $Z_{2}^{1/2}$, so the renormalised charge is $e = Z_{3}^{1/2}e_{0}$ and the insertion is the running $\alpha(s) = \alpha/(1 - \mathrm{Re}\,\hat\Pi(s))$ at this order (argued). All three clauses are standard \cite{LSZ1955, PeskinSchroeder1995}, recomputed in the spacetime algebra.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-bochner-theorem-momen.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] \emph{(Bochner.)} A function $f: \R \to \mathbb{C}$ is of the form $f(\tau) = \int e^{-i\nu\tau}\,d\mu(\nu)$ for a finite positive Borel measure $\mu$ on $\R$ exactly when it is continuous and positive definite ($\sum_{j,l}\bar c_{j}c_{l}f(\tau_{l} - \tau_{j}) \ge 0$ for all finite families); then $\mu(\R) = f(0)$.
\item[(b)] \emph{(The moment lemma; Lukacs, ch.~2.)} If $\mu$ is a finite positive Borel measure on $\R$ and its Fourier transform $f$ has a derivative of order $2k$ at $\tau = 0$, then $\int \nu^{2k}\,d\mu(\nu) < \infty$; then $f$ is of class $C^{2k}$ and $f^{(2k)}(0) = (-1)^{k}\int\nu^{2k}\,d\mu$.
\item[(c)] \emph{(Whitney.)} If $f$ is a smooth even function on an open interval symmetric about $0$, there is a smooth $g$ with $f(x) = g(x^{2})$; if $f$ is smooth and odd, $f(x) = x\,g(x^{2})$ with $g$ smooth (apply the even case to $f(x)/x$, smooth and even by Hadamard's lemma).
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Bochner1933}; \cite[ch.~2]{Lukacs1970}; \cite{Whitney1943}.

synthesis.imported-brst-kugo-ojima-stroc.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(BRST symmetry.)} The gauge-fixed action of a gauge theory in a covariant gauge, with Faddeev-Popov ghosts $c^{a}$, antighosts $\bar c_{a}$ and auxiliary fields, is invariant under a nilpotent fermionic symmetry $s$, $s^{2} = 0$, which acts on the gauge field as a gauge transformation with parameter $c$ and on the ghosts as $sc = -\tfrac12[c, c]$; its Noether charge $Q_{B}$ is nilpotent, and the physical states are the cohomology $\ker Q_{B}/\operatorname{im}Q_{B}$ \cite{BecchiRouetStora1976, Tyutin1975}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Quartet mechanism.)} In the local covariant operator formalism the state space carries an indefinite inner product for which $Q_{B}$ is self-adjoint; the physical subspace $\ker Q_{B}$ has a positive semi-definite inner product whose null space is $\operatorname{im}Q_{B}$, the unphysical modes (the timelike and longitudinal polarisations, ghosts and antighosts) forming BRST quartets of zero norm, so that the quotient is a Hilbert space and the physical scattering matrix is unitary \cite{KugoOjima1979}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Strocchi's theorem.)} A local, Lorentz-covariant vector potential $A_{\mu}$ realised on a Hilbert space with positive metric, whose field tensor obeys Maxwell's equations, has a field tensor with identically vanishing two-point function; a local covariant quantisation of a gauge field with nonzero field strength therefore needs an indefinite metric, as in the Gupta-Bleuler formalism \cite{Strocchi1967, Strocchi1970, Gottschalk2005}.
\item[(iv)] \emph{(The Gribov ambiguity.)} For a non-abelian gauge group the Coulomb (and the Landau) gauge condition does not fix the gauge uniquely: large field configurations carry several gauge-equivalent copies obeying the condition, so the Faddeev-Popov procedure is valid only perturbatively \cite{Gribov1978}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-conformal-covariance-.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on four-dimensional flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) with smooth derived metric $g$ on an open region $U$, and read the Weyl curvature as the Weyl part of the field strength (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-gtg-dictionary}).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] \emph{(Local conformal flatness; Weyl, Schouten.)} If the Weyl curvature vanishes on $U$, every point of $U$ has a neighbourhood and a gauge in which $\hb(b) = b/\varphi(x)$ with $\varphi$ smooth and positive, so that the derived metric there is $\varphi^{2}$ times the flat one; conversely such a field has vanishing Weyl curvature. (In dimension at least four, a metric is locally conformally flat exactly when its Weyl tensor vanishes.)
\item[(b)] \emph{(Conformal covariance.)} Let $\tilde g = \varphi^{2}g$ with $\varphi$ smooth and positive on $U$, and use the rescaled frame for spinors. Then $\phi$ solves the conformally coupled massless equation (the wave equation with the coupling to the scalar curvature at one sixth, in the sign that makes it conformally covariant) exactly when $\tilde\phi = \varphi^{-1}\phi$ solves it for $\tilde g$; $\psi$ solves the massless Dirac equation for $g$ exactly when $\tilde\psi = \varphi^{-3/2}\psi$ solves it for $\tilde g$; and the source-free Maxwell and Yang-Mills equations for a gauge potential $A$ are the same for $g$ and $\tilde g$, with $A$ unchanged. These are bijections of the solution spaces; the Klein-Gordon symplectic form and the conserved Dirac current are preserved, so they identify the CCR and CAR algebras of the two fields.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Weyl1918}, \cite{Schouten1921} (clause (a)); \cite[Appendix~D]{Wald1984} (the scalar and the Maxwell field), \cite[Sec.~5.7]{PenroseRindler1984} (massless free fields, the Dirac rescaling among them). Massive fields, and the minimally coupled massless scalar off fields with $R = 0$, are not conformally covariant and are not part of this Fact.

synthesis.imported-dimock-dirac-car-alge.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on four-dimensional flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) whose derived metric is globally hyperbolic, read on several charts where its global structure needs them, and consider the free Dirac field of mass $m_{0} \ge 0$ on it.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] \emph{(The field algebra.)} (Dimock) There are field operators $\psi(f)$ satisfying the Dirac equation with a causal anticommutator: $\{\psi(f), \psi(h)^{*}\}$ is the pairing given by the causal propagator of the Dirac operator and vanishes when the supports of $f$ and $h$ are spacelike separated. Formulated through the C$^{*}$-algebras of local observables (the CAR algebras of regions), the construction is free of ambiguity, and the net of these algebras satisfies a generalised form of the Haag-Kastler axioms (isotony, causality, covariance under the isometries of the field). No state is selected.
\item[(b)] \emph{(Reproducing formula; Dimock, Prop.~2.4, Sec.~III, Thm.~3.1.)} Let $S_{\pm}$ be the retarded and advanced fundamental solutions of the Dirac operator and $S = S_{+} - S_{-}$ the causal propagator. For a Cauchy surface $\Sigma$ with future unit normal $n$, the inner product of solutions $(\Phi, \Phi')$, the polarisation of the flux $\int_{\Sigma}J_{\Phi}\cdot n\,d\Sigma$ of the conserved current $J_{\Phi} = \Phi e_{0}\tilde\Phi$, does not depend on $\Sigma$; every solution is reproduced from its Cauchy data by $S$, $\Phi(x) = \int_{\Sigma}S(x, x')\,n(x')\,\Phi(x')\,d\Sigma'$ (in the source's normalisation $u = -iSp'\gamma u_{0}$, with $p'$ the adjoint of the restriction to $\Sigma$; the store normalises $S$ so that no factor $-i$ appears); and the anticommutator pairing of (a) is the inner product of the solutions the test spinors define, $\{\psi(f), \psi(h)^{*}\} = (Sf, Sh)$, with $(Sf, \Phi) = \int f^{\dagger}\Phi$ for smooth solutions $\Phi$.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Dimock1982}. \emph{Beyond one flat chart.} Global hyperbolicity and the Cauchy surfaces are global; the algebra of a region is read on one chart.

synthesis.imported-free-field-spin-stati.formal:
Signature $(+,-,-,-)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(The massless two-point function.)} The vacuum two-point function of the free massless scalar field is $D_{+}(\xi) = -\dfrac{1}{4\pi^{2}\bigl(Q(\xi) - i0\,\xi_{0}\bigr)}$; on the open spacelike set $\{Q(\xi) < 0\}$ it is the real function $-1/(4\pi^{2}Q(\xi))$, even under $\xi \to -\xi$ \cite{Shalabi2024, StreaterWightman1964}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Pauli's argument.)} For a free field whose particle and antiparticle parts carry a polarisation sum that is a polynomial $P(k)$ homogeneous of degree $d$, the commutator or anticommutator at spacelike separation is a multiple of $(1 \pm (-1)^{d})P(\partial)D_{+}$; with positive metric, locality forces commutators for integer spin and anticommutators for half-integer spin \cite{Pauli1940}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Guido and Longo.)} Let a net of field algebras on the spacelike cones of Minkowski space have the Reeh-Schlieder property and normal commutation relations (Bose and Fermi parts graded by a self-adjoint unitary $\Gamma$ fixing the vacuum), and be modular covariant: the modular group of the vacuum for each wedge algebra acts as the wedge's boosts, rescaled by $-2\pi$. Then the net carries a positive-energy representation $U$ of the covering group of the Poincar\'e group, and $\Gamma = U(2\pi)$ \cite{GuidoLongo1995}.
\end{enumerate}

