synthesis.imported-kuckert-modular-pct-s.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Kuckert.)} Let $\mathcal{A}$ be a net of local observables on Minkowski space in its vacuum sector $\mathcal{H}_{0}$ with vacuum $\Omega$. Assume: (I) $\mathcal{H}_{0}$ carries a strongly continuous unitary representation $U$ of the universal covering of the restricted Poincar\'e group under which $\mathcal{A}$ is covariant and $\Omega$ invariant, and the joint spectrum of the generators of the translations lies in the closed forward cone; (II) modular P$_{1}$CT symmetry: for the standard wedge $W$, the modular conjugation $J$ of $(\mathcal{A}(W), \Omega)$ satisfies $J\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})J = \mathcal{A}(j\mathcal{O})$ for every double cone $\mathcal{O}$, with $j$ the reflection reversing the two coordinates of the boost plane of $W$ (P$_{1}$T); (III) the group of internal symmetries is compact in the strong operator topology. Then, with $r(\varphi)$ the rotations in the spatial plane spanned by the boost direction of $W$ and one edge direction, $W_{\varphi} = r(\varphi)W$, $J_{\varphi}$ the modular conjugation of $(\mathcal{A}(W_{\varphi}), \Omega)$ and $R(\varphi) = U(r(\varphi))$, $R(\varphi) = J_{\varphi/2}J$ (Corollary 3.2), so $R(2\pi) = 1$ on $\mathcal{H}_{0}$; and on the field algebra of the parabosonic and parafermionic charges of finite statistics, built by Doplicher and Roberts \cite{DoplicherRoberts1990} (twisted duality being their theorem, not an assumption), the rotation by $2\pi$ equals the Bose-Fermi operator $k$ (Theorem 3.4, Corollary 3.5): the spin-statistics connection \cite{Kuckert1995}. The modular groups $\Delta^{it}$ need not act geometrically on the net; the argument also works in $1 + 2$ dimensions.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Borchers.)} Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a von Neumann algebra with cyclic and separating vector $\Omega$ and modular objects $\Delta$, $J$, and let $U(s)$ be a continuous one-parameter unitary group with positive generator, $U(s)\Omega = \Omega$ for all $s$ and $U(s)\mathcal{M}U(s)^{*} \subset \mathcal{M}$ for $s \ge 0$. Then $\Delta^{it}U(s)\Delta^{-it} = U(e^{-2\pi t}s)$ and $JU(s)J = U(-s)$ for all real $s$, $t$ \cite{Borchers1992, Summers2006}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-lsz-reduction-ward-id.formal:
Units $\hbar = c = 1$, signature $(+,-,-,-)$, $\alpha = e^{2}/(4\pi)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Reduction formula.)} For a field theory with asymptotic in and out fields normalised by a constant $Z$, an S-matrix element is the residue at the one-particle poles $Q(p_{j}) = m_{j}^{2}$ of the Fourier transform of the time-ordered function of the interpolating fields, amputated, with one factor $Z^{1/2}$ per external leg \cite{LSZ1955}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Ward's identity.)} In electrodynamics the vertex function at zero momentum transfer is minus the derivative of the electron self-energy with respect to the electron momentum, $\Lambda^{\mu}(p, p) = -\partial\Sigma(p)/\partial p_{\mu}$; hence the vertex and the electron wave-function renormalisation constants agree, $Z_{1} = Z_{2}$, to all orders, and the charge is renormalised by the photon constant alone, $e = Z_{3}^{1/2}e_{0}$ \cite{Ward1950}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Two-loop charge renormalisation.)} For $n_{f}$ Dirac fermions of unit charge, in a mass-independent scheme, $\mu\,d\alpha/d\mu = \frac{2n_{f}}{3\pi}\alpha^{2} + \frac{n_{f}}{2\pi^{2}}\alpha^{3} + O(\alpha^{4})$; with $a = \alpha/(4\pi)$, $\beta_{0} = \tfrac43 n_{f}$ and $\beta_{1} = 4n_{f}$, and the massless two-loop photon renormalisation constant is $Z_{3} = 1 - \tfrac43 n_{f}a/\epsilon - 2n_{f}a^{2}/\epsilon$ in $d = 4 - 2\epsilon$ \cite{JostLuttinger1950, Steinhauser2002}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-quasi-spin-pairing-an.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Quasi-spin solution of degenerate pairing.)} For fermions in one level of pair degeneracy $\Omega$ with the pairing Hamiltonian $H = -GS^{+}S^{-}$, $S^{+} = \sum_{m > 0}a^{*}_{m}a^{*}_{\bar m}$ and $S^{-} = (S^{+})^{*}$, the operators $S^{\pm}$ and $S_{z} = \tfrac12(\hat N - \Omega)$ form an $\su(2)$ (quasi-spin), and the energies are $E(n, v) = -\tfrac{G}{4}(n - v)(2\Omega - n - v + 2)$ for $n$ particles of seniority $v$ (the number of unpaired particles); so a state of $p$ pairs with $v = 0$ has $E = -Gp(\Omega - p + 1)$ and breaking one pair costs $E(n, 2) - E(n, 0) = G\Omega$ \cite{Kerman1961, AgrawalMaheshwari2020}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Nambu and Jona-Lasinio.)} In the four-fermion model with dynamical mass $m$, at leading order the scalar ($\sigma$) bound state lies at $m_{\sigma} = 2m$, the threshold of pair breaking, beside the massless pseudoscalar \cite{NambuJonaLasinio1961}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Top condensation.)} If the electroweak symmetry is broken by a top-quark condensate, the composite Higgs boson has $m_{h} = 2m_{t}$ at leading order in $1/N_{c}$, lowered by gauge and Higgs corrections \cite{BardeenHillLindner1990}; a model with top and bottom condensates and anomalous $U(1)_{A}$ breaking obtains a composite Higgs mass in accord with the measured $125$ GeV \cite{Osipov2019}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-schwinger-term-and-ma.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(The density algebra.)} For one chiral band of fermion modes $a_{k}$, $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, with the levels $k \le 0$ filled in the vacuum, the normal-ordered density modes $\rho(p) = \sum_{k}{:}a^{*}_{k+p}a_{k}{:}$ obey $[\rho(-p), \rho(p')] = p\,\delta_{pp'}$ for $p > 0$: up to normalisation, $b_{p} = \rho(-p)/\sqrt{p}$ and $b_{p}^{\dagger}$ are canonical boson modes \cite{Tomonaga1950, MattisLieb1965, vonDelftSchoeller1998}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Its origin.)} The central term $p$ is absent from the commutator of the unordered densities of any finite band (there $[\rho(-p), \rho(p)]$ is a difference of occupation numbers, of zero vacuum expectation in total); it arises from the infinitely filled sea through normal ordering \cite{MattisLieb1965, vonDelftSchoeller1998}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The Schwinger term.)} In a relativistic field theory with a positive-energy vacuum, the equal-time commutator of the charge density with the current density cannot vanish: it carries a term, the Schwinger term, proportional to the derivative of a delta function, whose one-dimensional chiral form is the central term of (i) \cite{Schwinger1959}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-singer-gauge-slice-ne.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(No continuous gauge slice.)} For the gauge group $SU(N)$, $N \ge 2$, on the three-sphere or the four-sphere (a compactified spatial slice or a compactified Euclidean spacetime), the space of connections does not admit a continuous gauge-fixing condition: there is no global continuous section of the bundle of connections over the gauge orbits, so every continuous gauge condition has Gribov copies \cite{Singer1978, EspositoPellicciaZaccaria2004}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Lattice BRST gives 0/0.)} On a finite lattice with a compact gauge group, the BRST gauge-fixed integral of a BRST-invariant observable is a topological invariant: the Gribov copies on each orbit are summed with the signs of the Faddeev-Popov determinant, giving the Euler characteristic of the gauge group, which is zero for $SU(n)$ (and for every compact connected Lie group of positive dimension); so the partition function vanishes and expectation values are $0/0$ \cite{Neuberger1987, KalloniatisVonSmekalWilliams2005}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Complementarity.)} In a lattice gauge theory with Higgs fields of fixed length in the fundamental representation of the gauge group, the Higgs region and the confinement region of the phase diagram are analytically connected, with no phase boundary between them; for Higgs fields in other representations (the adjoint, or charge two for $U(1)$) a boundary can exist; a free-charge phase is generally present \cite{FradkinShenker1979}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-wilson-lattice-gauge-.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Wilson's lattice gauge theory.)} On a hypercubic lattice with link variables $U_{\ell}$ in a compact group $G$ and plaquette products $U_{p}$, the weight $\exp(-\beta\sum_{p}(1 - \tfrac1N\mathrm{Re}\,\mathrm{Tr}\,U_{p}))$ with the Haar measure on each link defines a gauge-invariant theory with no gauge fixing; for $G = SU(N)$, $\beta = 2N/g^{2}$, so $\beta = 4/g^{2}$ for $SU(2)$, and as the lattice spacing goes to zero the weight reduces to the Yang-Mills action \cite{Wilson1974, Denbleyker2008}. At strong coupling (small $\beta$) the expectation of a Wilson loop decreases as the exponential of minus the area it encloses, at leading order in $\beta$ \cite{Wilson1974}.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Reflection positivity and the area law.)} Wilson's lattice gauge theories, with or without fermions, satisfy reflection (physical) positivity, which gives a Hilbert space and a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix; at strong coupling the infinite-volume limit exists and is analytic in $\beta$, and Wilson loops obey the area law (Wilson's confinement bound) \cite{OsterwalderSeiler1978}.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Elitzur's theorem.)} A local gauge symmetry cannot be broken spontaneously in a gauge-invariant lattice theory without gauge fixing: gauge-variant observables have vanishing expectation \cite{Elitzur1975}.
\item[(iv)] \emph{(Compact $U(1)$ deconfines.)} Four-dimensional compact $U(1)$ lattice gauge theory has, at weak coupling, a phase without confinement of electric charge, a massless phase of quantum electrodynamics, separated by a phase transition from its confining strong-coupling phase; proved for a Villain-type action by Guth and by Fr\"ohlich and Spencer \cite{Guth1980, FrohlichSpencer1982}.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.krein-form-premise-indefinite-.formal:
\emph{Premise (KF), the Krein form for gauge fields.} Units and signature of the store. Let the polarisation modes $a_{\mu} = \partial/\partial z_{\mu}$ of a gauge field act on the palatial polynomial representation (Fact \claimref{field-theory.imported-statistics-no-go-palatial-algebra}) with its positive form $\langle z^{n}, z^{m}\rangle = n!\,\delta_{nm}$, and let ghost modes act on tower floors (Fact \claimref{field-theory.imported-tower-limit}) with their positive trace form. The premise: \emph{the state space of the series' gauge fields, with their ghosts and their regulator fields, carries an indefinite (Krein) inner product $\langle x, y\rangle_{K} = \langle x, \eta y\rangle$, $\eta$ a self-adjoint involution of the positive form (the fundamental symmetry), such that}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(K1)] \emph{(Gupta-Bleuler form.)} on one polarisation quantum $[a(u), a(w)^{\dagger}] = -u\cdot w$ for polarisation vectors $u$, $w$: the one-particle form is the Minkowski metric of the polarisations, invariant under the Lorentz rotors (realised by $\eta = (-1)^{n_{0}}$ on the polynomials);
\item[(K2)] \emph{(Ghosts.)} the ghost and antighost modes of a covariant gauge are tower modes whose form is twisted by the involution exchanging the two modes of a floor, as in Theorem \claimref{synthesis.self-adjoint-brst-charge-needs-a-krein-form-and-the-tower-supplies-the-ghosts}(ii);
\item[(K3)] \emph{(Regulators.)} Pauli-Villars regulator fields of weight $c_{i} < 0$ are states of negative norm for the same form, so that regulated loops may carry weights $\sum_{i}c_{i} = \sum_{i}c_{i}M_{i}^{2} = 0$ (Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-one-loop-integrals-and-pauli-villars-regulators});
\item[(K4)] \emph{(Positive physical space.)} the BRST charge $Q_{B}$ is nilpotent and self-adjoint for $\langle\,,\rangle_{K}$, the physical space is its cohomology $\ker Q_{B}/\operatorname{im}Q_{B}$ at ghost number zero, and the form is positive definite there: the unphysical modes (timelike and longitudinal polarisations, ghosts, antighosts) occur only in zero-norm quartets (Kugo and Ojima, Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-brst-kugo-ojima-strocchi-and-gribov}), and physical amplitudes are taken between physical states, from which the regulator fields decouple as their masses go to infinity.
\end{enumerate}

\emph{What it is for.} One premise covers two standard uses of an indefinite metric that the store's positive representations cannot carry. (a) Covariant gauge fixing: a nonzero self-adjoint nilpotent BRST charge does not exist on a positive space (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.self-adjoint-brst-charge-needs-a-krein-form-and-the-tower-supplies-the-ghosts}(i)), and a local covariant vector potential obeying Maxwell's equations has no positive-metric realisation with nonzero field strength (Strocchi, Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-brst-kugo-ojima-strocchi-and-gribov}); with (KF) the covariant photon propagator $-g_{\mu\nu}/k^{2}$ may be used on internal lines and the background Feynman gauge with Faddeev-Popov ghosts may be used in loops. (b) Covariant regularisation: Pauli-Villars fields of negative weight. Where the store's photon (Proposition \claimref{field-theory.elementary-photon-ga}(4)) gives the covariant form only at tree level on conserved currents, (KF) is what licenses it inside loops.

\emph{Results that use it} (each states it as a hypothesis at every level and has an assumes edge to it): one-loop electrodynamics in the spacetime algebra, in a covariant gauge with Pauli-Villars regulators (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.one-loop-qed-in-the-spacetime-algebra}); the one-loop running coefficients in the background Feynman gauge (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.one-loop-running-coefficients-from-spacetime-algebra-spin-traces}); the self-adjoint BRST charge with tower ghosts and its positive cohomology at a null momentum (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.self-adjoint-brst-charge-needs-a-krein-form-and-the-tower-supplies-the-ghosts}, whose clauses (ii) to (v) are computations in the form (K1), (K2) and are read as the series' gauge fixing under (KF)). By the decision of 2 November 2026 the following also carry an assumes edge to it, each stating it as the hypothesis of a clause: the two-loop vacuum polarisation in dimensional regularisation (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.two-loop-qed-in-the-spacetime-algebra-continued-in-dimension-gives-the-second-beta-coefficient}, its covariant internal photon); the free positivity of gluon and massive-vector physical states at every number of quanta (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quartet-homotopy-makes-gluon-and-massive-vector-physical-states-positive-at-every-number-of-quanta}, clauses (K1), (K2)); the annihilation of an electron-positron pair into muons (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.electron-positron-to-muon-pairs-in-the-spacetime-algebra-lsz-tree-cross-section-and-one-loop-unitarity}, only its charge-renormalisation sentence); the regulator-free vacuum polarisation (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.one-loop-vacuum-polarisation-extends-without-a-regulator-and-ward-preserving-extensions-differ-only-by-the-renormalisation-constants}, only the self-energy and vertex parts of its clause (iii); its vacuum polarisation uses neither (KF) nor a regulator); the running of the rung-field quartics (\claimref{synthesis.rung-field-quartics-run-to-strong-coupling-within-a-few-tev-on-the-transmutation-branch}, its gauge terms); the Krein quartet in the induced Einstein-Hilbert coefficient (\claimref{synthesis.krein-quartet-drops-from-the-induced-einstein-hilbert-coefficient-only-under-a-regulator-that-treats-it-alike}); the gauge parts of the one-loop coefficients of the slot-module result (\claimref{synthesis.slot-module-makes-the-faces-separate-composites-and-leaves-the-generation-number-free}); and the interacting spin-statistics of causal perturbation theory (\claimref{synthesis.interacting-fields-of-causal-perturbation-theory-keep-the-spin-statistics-connection-at-even-ghost-number}). In the locality analysis of the attached free fields the Krein case $c = -1$ is the stated exception in which an anticommuting scalar, the ghost, is local (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.locality-of-the-attached-free-fields-fixes-their-statistics}(2)); that result does not rest on (KF).

\emph{What it does not give.} It does not derive the form from transport or from any stored structure: it is the standard Gupta-Bleuler form, transport-absent. The indefinite twistor form of signature $(2, 2)$ on $\CS$ (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.positive-structure-is-twistor-form-at-imaginary-time}) is a parallel at the one-particle level of the attachment, not this form. Positivity of the physical space is computed in the store only for the free charge at a null momentum up to three quanta; positivity for every number of quanta is the quartet mechanism (imported), and for the interacting non-abelian theory, with the field-level BRST charge and beyond perturbation theory where Gribov copies arise, it is not established. It supplies no native Lorentz-covariant regulator: the Pauli-Villars fields stay imported fields, and the series' own floors are a frame-dependent cut-off (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.floor-regulator-keeps-the-charge-and-the-central-term-below-the-cut-off}). It says nothing about confinement.

\emph{Note (2 November 2026).} (a) Clause (K3) is not needed for the one-loop vacuum polarisation: by the extension of distributions it extends across the coincident point with no regulator field and no Krein form, to the stored log coefficient (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.one-loop-vacuum-polarisation-extends-without-a-regulator-and-ward-preserving-extensions-differ-only-by-the-renormalisation-constants}(ii)). (K3) is not retired: the self-energy and vertex clauses of the stored one-loop electrodynamics still use Pauli-Villars fields, and their regulator-free extensions are imported, not computed. (b) The one-particle form $-u\cdot w$ of (K1) is restated inside the store: it is the trace form $\langle\bar a b\rangle_{0} = -a\cdot b$ of Clifford conjugation on vectors, minus the store's metric in any Clifford algebra, and Clifford conjugation is the adjoint of the twistor form (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.clifford-conjugation-is-the-twistor-adjoint-and-its-trace-form-is-the-one-particle-gupta-bleuler-form}). This restates and does not derive (K1): that the polarisation and ghost state spaces are quantised with the indefinite form remains this premise. (c) Given (K1) and (K2), the free physical space is positive at every number of quanta for gluons and, in the Feynman-'t Hooft gauge, for massive vectors with their Goldstone modes (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.quartet-homotopy-makes-gluon-and-massive-vector-physical-states-positive-at-every-number-of-quanta}); positivity for the interacting charge stays within (K4), and the covariant BRST construction is perturbative (Gribov copies and the vanishing lattice BRST partition function, Theorem \claimref{synthesis.non-abelian-gauge-fixing-has-gribov-copies-and-the-weak-rotor-lattice-with-doublets-has-a-perimeter-law}).

\emph{Refutation condition.} The premise is false for the series if any of the following is established: (a) for the series' gauge fields the physical space defined in (K4) contains a state of negative norm, or a null state not in $\operatorname{im}Q_{B}$, in a regime where the theory is meant to apply (failure of the quartet mechanism, for example for the interacting non-abelian charge), so that the physical scattering matrix is not unitary; (b) physical amplitudes computed under it depend, after the regulators are removed, on the gauge parameter or on the regulator weights; (c) a derivation from the series' premises that every state space of the series, unphysical modes included, carries a positive form, which (KF) contradicts.

synthesis.locality-of-the-attached-free-.formal:
Let $f(\xi) = 1/Q(\xi)$, and take the massless two-point function $D_{+}$, which on the open spacelike set equals $-f/(4\pi^{2})$ and is even under $\xi \to -\xi$ (standard; Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-free-field-spin-statistics-and-guido-longo-theorem}(i)). For an attached free field with particle and antiparticle parts carrying a polarisation sum $P$, a polynomial in $k$ homogeneous of degree $d$, with weights $1$ and $c \neq 0$ ($c > 0$ when the field's Fock space has a positive norm), and with bracket sign $\eta = +1$ (CAR, anticommutator) or $-1$ (CCR, commutator):
\begin{enumerate}
\item The stored fields' sums are $P = 1$ (scalar, $d = 0$), $P(k) = k\gamma_{0} = 2k_{0}\Lambda(k)$ with $\Lambda$ the projector of Proposition \claimref{field-theory.second-quantisation} ($d = 1$), and for the Riemann-Silberstein photon of Proposition \claimref{field-theory.fock-space-maxwell-modes}, on bivectors $A$, $B$, $P(k)[A, B] = \sum_{\epsilon}\langle A(k \wedge \epsilon)\rangle_{0}\langle B(k \wedge \epsilon)\rangle_{0}$ over transverse $\epsilon$, which on the null cone equals the quadratic $-\langle A(k \wedge \gamma_{0})\rangle_{0}\langle B(k \wedge \gamma_{0})\rangle_{0} + \sum_{i}\langle A(k \wedge \gamma_{i})\rangle_{0}\langle B(k \wedge \gamma_{i})\rangle_{0}$ ($d = 2$). So $d = 2s$ and $P(-k) = (-1)^{2s}P(k)$.
\item At spacelike $\xi$ the bracket equals $-\tfrac{I_{4}^{d}}{4\pi^{2}}\bigl(1 + \eta c(-1)^{d}\bigr)P(\partial)f(\xi)$, and $P(\partial)f$ is nonzero there for each of the three sums ($-2\xi\gamma_{0}/Q(\xi)^{2}$ for the spinor). Hence, with positive norm ($c > 0$), the field is local exactly when $c = 1$ and $\eta = -(-1)^{2s}$; with an indefinite (Krein) form and $c = -1$ it is local exactly when $\eta = (-1)^{2s}$, so an anticommuting scalar is then local (the Faddeev-Popov ghost case).
\item The tower plane ($Q(v) = Q(w) = 0$, $\{v, w\} = 1$, Fact \claimref{field-theory.imported-tower-limit}) has $\eta = +1$; the palatial pair on polynomials has $\eta = -1$. So, with positive norm, a CAR (tower) lift is local only for the spinor and a CCR (palatial) lift only for the scalar and the photon; with Proposition \claimref{field-theory.no-canonical-commutation-relations-tower}, an elementary local scalar of positive norm, such as a quantised rung field, is palatial.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.one-loop-qed-in-the-spacetime-.formal:
